"""DataCamp worked examples — chapter 1 (one beginner walk-through per lesson).

Shared content for scripts/apply_datacamp_example.py. Each entry is
an original worked example in plain register: a concrete scenario the
learner can follow before attempting the lesson itself. Keyed by the
companion note's slug; the apply script inserts the section between
the beats and the through-lines.
"""

EXAMPLES_1 = {
    "ch01-01-security-risks-specific-to-ai": """A small retailer launches an AI support chatbot. Walk the five risks past it, one by one, by asking where the fault would enter. **Bias**: the bot was trained on two years of resolved tickets; if past agents escalated certain customer names slowly, the bot learns that pattern — the fault entered through the training data. **Hallucination**: it confidently cites a return policy clause that does not exist — the fault is in generation, with no attacker and no bad data. **Data poisoning**: a competitor files fake tickets praising a rival product until the bot recommends it — the fault entered through training data an attacker controlled. **Adversarial input**: a user crafts a message that makes the bot override its guidelines — the fault entered at run time. **Sensitive data exposure**: the bot repeats a customer's card details back into a shared transcript — the fault is in data handling. The retailer's first lesson is the one this repository's [Ch. 01](../../chapters/01-risk-models-and-research-epistemics/README.md) formalizes: name the phase before naming the risk, because each phase implies a different fix.""",
    "ch01-02-ai-risk-awareness": """Three statements, sorted by phase. *Statement A:* "the model began recommending a product no genuine reviewer liked." Something in the training data was planted — data poisoning. *Statement B:* "the model confidently described a refund procedure that does not exist." No attacker needed; the model fabricated and asserted — hallucination. *Statement C:* "a carefully worded message made the assistant ignore its own rules." The model itself is unchanged; the input was crafted at run time — adversarial. The drill's trap is that all three end in "the bot did something wrong." The discriminating question is always the same one: *when and where did the fault enter?* Training data, generation, or run-time input. Answer that, and the family names itself — the same discipline [Ch. 01](../../chapters/01-risk-models-and-research-epistemics/README.md) builds into threat hypotheses.""",
    "ch01-03-dinner-at-riskland-junction": """One round of the game, played slowly. The story beat: "After the update, diners at the Junction started receiving dessert recommendations for a bakery that paid for placement — the regulars stopped trusting the menu." The harm word is *deceived*, which tempts you toward "hallucination." Run the two-step instead. First, the phase: did the fault enter through training data, run-time input, generation, or data handling? The recommendations changed after the update, following planted signals in the interaction data — training time. Second, the family: training-data manipulation by an interested party is **data poisoning**. The cost of a misread is real: call it hallucination and you retrain the model; call it poisoning and you audit the data supply chain — only one of those fixes the actual hole.""",
    "ch01-04-ai-as-a-change-agent-for-security": """A mid-size bank wants machine help against wire fraud. Match by object, not by vendor. The object is *events* — thousands of transfers per day, each a record to correlate — so the first family is **SIEM**: it ingests the event stream and flags anomalies, like transfers to rarely used accounts in high-risk countries at unusual hours. Add **AI** and the SIEM learns from past fraud attempts: it can score each transfer in real time, faster than manual review, and hand the top suspects to a human investigator. If instead the bank's pain were *workflows* — analysts copy-pasting between five tools — the answer would be **SOAR**. If it were *behavior* — spotting a compromised account by how it acts — **UEBA**. If it were *adversary intelligence* — who attacks banks like this, and why — a **TIP**. The course's bank example is the SIEM path; your job in the exercise is to hear which object the stem describes and name the family.""",
    "ch01-05-enhancing-security-operations-with-ai": """Three needs, three families. *Need 1:* "alert me when an employee's account starts behaving like someone else's" — the object is behavior over time: **UEBA**. *Need 2:* "tell me which tactics the group targeting our sector is using next" — the object is adversary intelligence: a **TIP**. *Need 3:* "when an alert fires, automatically open a ticket, isolate the host, and page the on-call analyst" — the object is the workflow itself: **SOAR**. The distractor answers all sound plausible because every family says "analyze" and "alert." The tie-breaker is the *object of the analysis*: behavior, adversary, or workflow. If a stem describes what is being studied rather than which product category it belongs to, you already have your answer.""",
    "ch01-06-risks-of-ai-in-security": """The model flags a transaction as fraudulent but cannot say why, and the analyst asks who owns the decision. The honest answer has two halves. *Accountability half:* responsibility follows the inspectable path — since it is difficult to understand how the system decided, it is hard to know who is responsible; that is the video's exact warning, and it is why the answer pattern is "whoever could foresee and mitigate," which requires the decision path to be reviewable. *Ethics half:* suppose the same bank adds face recognition to branch cameras. The security benefit is real, and so are the fairness and privacy costs the video names — unfair results for specific groups, privacy concerns. The answer that scores states BOTH sides before concluding; the answer that carries only one side is the marked-wrong one.""",
    "ch01-07-security-catalyst-station": """The station hands you one situation: "The security team is drowning in alerts from forty systems; leadership wants fewer, better alerts, and the analysts want their weekends back." Run the four-step. (1) *Object:* events across systems — this is correlation, not behavior or adversary intelligence. (2) *Latency:* real-time flags, not hindsight. (3) *Explainability:* the flagged event must be reviewable by a human, or accountability dies. (4) *Weighing:* the benefit must survive the fairness and privacy costs of watching systems at all. The defensible answer is an AI-assisted **SIEM** for correlation, feeding a **SOAR** for the repetitive responses — freeing the analysts for the sophisticated threats, exactly the division the video draws. A wrong catalyst is not a small error: automating around undetected events, or studying your own users while the adversary walks past, is the failure mode the game prices.""",
    "ch01-08-the-ai-security-ecosystem": """A company launches an AI hiring-assist tool. Watch each pillar raise its requirement. *People:* recruiters want speed; the data scientist asks whether the model is precise enough; legal asks what the model's decisions mean for candidate privacy; the executive asks about return and reputation — the video's three perspectives, needing reconciliation, not a winner. *Legal:* regulators are scrambling; meeting today's rules is insufficient, so the company commits to fairness, accountability, and transparency ahead of mandate — the emerging trend the video names. *Technology:* the model serves from cloud infrastructure that must resist hacking and unauthorized access while staying agile. *Processes:* bias testing on a cadence, incident response planned, continuous monitoring — the practices that keep security consistent over time. The lesson a beginner should keep: the tool is one pillar of four, and the launch fails if any pillar is a blank.""",
    "ch01-09-the-importance-of-multiple-viewpoints": """Three quotes from one meeting. *"The model hits 94 percent precision on the test set."* — that is the data scientist; the claim is about the model. *"If it auto-rejects candidates, we are exposed under fairness law."* — legal; the claim is about the decisions' implications. *"What is the payback period, and what happens to our brand if this goes wrong?"* — the executive; the claim is about return and reputation. The exercise wants you to attribute the speaker BEFORE evaluating the claim, because an unattributed claim cannot be reconciled with the others — it just competes. And the deliverable, per the video, is the reconciliation: a decision record in which each legitimate concern survives. The repository's own rule for the same move is recorded in [Ch. 01](../../chapters/01-risk-models-and-research-epistemics/README.md): record disagreement; do not average it away.""",
    "ch01-10-balancing-stakeholder-interests": """The tension: the data science team wants to log every candidate interaction for training data; legal says retention itself is a privacy risk. The balanced answer follows a fixed shape. *State both interests:* richer training data improves the model; stored personal data creates exposure the company would have to answer for. *Name the trade-off accepted:* say the company keeps the data — then it must say what it now owes. *State the mitigation for the side that lost ground:* minimize what is stored (keep features, drop identifiers), bound the retention period, and log the decision. Single-interest answers read as advocacy, not analysis — which is exactly what this exercise marks wrong. The pattern is the everyday version of what [Ch. 23](../../chapters/23-governance-and-standards/README.md) institutionalizes as governance instruments.""",
    "ch01-11-ecosystem-nexus": """One decision, all four pillars. The scenario: "The company will let the hiring tool auto-reject the bottom decile." *People:* recruiters gain speed; rejected candidates lose a human reader — whose concerns are engaged? *Legal:* automated adverse decisions are exactly what regulators watch — which expectations apply? *Technology:* can the system explain each rejection, and is the infrastructure protected from tampering with the model? *Processes:* is there a bias test on a cadence, an incident plan, and monitoring for drift? The game scores whether you touched every pillar your decision implicates — a choice that engages three pillars and answers one is the losing move. The nexus lesson in one line: at the center, decisions are never only technical, only legal, or only social; they are all three at once.""",
}
